extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Chart types | Scatter"
	- pagedescription = "Scatter and bubble charts in Tablechart: two numbers per row, as a marker with its name, on two axes."
	- const varieties = [['Elstar', '62', '1.20', '420', 'Eating'], ['Jonagold', '75', '1.05', '310', 'Eating'], ['Braeburn', '48', '1.45', '180', 'Eating'], ['Gala', '55', '1.30', '260', 'Eating'], ['Cox', '35', '1.85', '120', 'Eating'], ['Discovery', '30', '1.75', '60', 'Eating'], ['Boskoop', '70', '0.95', '200', 'Cooking'], ['Granny Smith', '40', '1.60', '90', 'Cooking'], ['Bramley', '70', '0.95', '140', 'Cooking']]

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Scatter
				p.lead Two numbers per row, as a marker with its name.

	+section("Scatter chart")
		p Each row is a marker. The first value column is the position across, the second the position up. The headers of those two columns are the titles of the axes. The name of the row is shown next to its marker.
		p The scatter chart is an extra. To use it, load it before the charts are made. See #[a(href="#load-it") Load it].

		+chart(null, '1,5')
			figure(data-chart="scatter")
				figcaption: b Harvest and price per variety
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Variety
							th Harvest per tree (kg)
							th Price per kg (€)
					tbody
						each row in varieties.slice(0, 6)
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		p The axes start a little below the lowest value and end a little above the highest value, so they do not always start at zero. The numbers along the axes have the same format as the numbers in the table, with the same text around them, such as #[code %].
		p If a row has no number in one of the two columns, it has no marker. The row stays in the table.

	+section("Bubbles")
		p If there is a third value column, it sets the size of each marker: the area of a marker matches its value. The largest marker is #[code --tablechart-bubble-max] wide, #[code 24px] by default. The header of the column is shown in the legend, with a small and a large circle.

		+chart(null, '5')
			figure(data-chart="scatter")
				figcaption: b Harvest, price and number of trees
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Variety
							th Harvest per tree (kg)
							th Price per kg (€)
							th Trees
					tbody
						each row in varieties.slice(0, 6)
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
								td= row[3]

		p If two markers overlap, the smaller one is on top.

	+section("Groups")
		p To show groups, add #[code data-group] to the header of a column with names, such as "Eating" and "Cooking". Each group then has its own colour, its own shape (a circle, a triangle, a square or a diamond) and an entry in the legend. The colours are #[code --tablechart-color-1], #[code --tablechart-color-2] and so on. See #[a(href=url('/styling/colours.html')) Colours].

		+chart(null, '5')
			figure.scatter-colours(data-chart="scatter")
				figcaption: b Eating and cooking apples
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Variety
							th Harvest per tree (kg)
							th Price per kg (€)
							th Trees
							th(data-group) Use
					tbody
						each row in varieties
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
								td= row[3]
								td= row[4]

		+style.
			.scatter-colours {
				--tablechart-color-1: #3f7fbf;
				--tablechart-color-2: #d9844f;
			}

		p The group names are cells of the table, so screen readers read them. If the figure has #[code data-chart-keys], each group name gets a translation key, such as #[code apples-group1]. See #[a(href=url('/features/translation.html')) Translation].

	+section("Names")
		p If there is room, a name is shown to the right of its marker. If not, it goes to the left, above, below, or to one of the corners. A name does not cover a marker or another name, and stays inside the axes. Larger markers get their place first.
		p If a name has no room at all, it is left out. The chart then has a tooltip: if you point at a marker, its name is shown. See #[a(href=url('/features/tooltips.html')) Tooltips].
		p If rows have the same two numbers, their markers are in the same place. They then share one name, with the number of the other rows, such as #[strong Boskoop +1] above. The tooltip of that marker shows all their names.
		p To turn the names off, add #[code data-chart-names="false"]. The tooltip then shows the name of the marker you point at. To turn them off on every scatter chart, set the #[code names] option to #[code false].

		+codeblock('HTML').
			<figure data-chart="scatter" data-chart-names="false">

		p If a chart has more than 400 markers, no names are placed. They are all left out, and the tooltip shows them.

	+section("Axes")
		p To set the axis up yourself, add #[code data-chart-scale]. If you give two numbers, they are where the axis starts and ends. If you give three or more, they are the numbers along the axis. #[code data-chart-scale-x] does the same for the axis across. #[code data-chart-scale-y] is another name for #[code data-chart-scale].

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="scatter" data-chart-scale-x="0 100" data-chart-scale="0 0.5 1 1.5 2")
				figcaption: b From zero
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Variety
							th Harvest per tree (kg)
							th Price per kg (€)
					tbody
						each row in varieties.slice(0, 6)
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		p If you do not give numbers, the axis goes up in steps of 1, 2 or 5 times 10, 100 and so on. If the chart is narrow, it shows fewer numbers, so they do not touch.

	+section("Gridlines")
		p To add a light line at each number along both axes, add #[code data-chart-grid]. To add them to every chart, set the #[code grid] option to #[code true].

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="scatter" data-chart-grid)
				figcaption: b With gridlines
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Variety
							th Harvest per tree (kg)
							th Price per kg (€)
					tbody
						each row in varieties.slice(0, 6)
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		p #[code --tablechart-grid-color], #[code --tablechart-grid-opacity] and #[code --tablechart-grid-width] set how the gridlines look. #[code --tablechart-axis-color], #[code --tablechart-axis-width] and #[code --tablechart-axis-size] set the axes and the text along them. See #[a(href=url('/styling/axes.html')) Axes and gridlines].

	+section("Load it")
		p The scatter chart is not in the main script. It is in the extras. See #[a(href=url('/types/extras.html')) Extras].

		+codeblock('JS').
			import { addType, init } from "@martinomagnifico/tablechart";
			import { scatter } from "@martinomagnifico/tablechart/extras";

			addType("scatter", scatter);
			init();

		p If you use a script tag, load #[code tablechart-extras.js] after #[code tablechart.js]. It adds the scatter chart.

		+codeblock('HTML').
			<script src="tablechart.js"></script>
			<script src="tablechart-extras.js"></script>
			<script>
				Tablechart.init();
			</script>

	+section("What it does not do")
		p A scatter chart has no steps, no stacks and no break in the scale. If a figure has one of those, the chart is shown whole. For a stack or a break, the console shows a warning. For steps, it shows a message if the #[code debug] option is on.

	+section("Next")
		p To set the colours of the groups, see #[a(href=url('/styling/colours.html')) Colours]. For all the attributes, see #[a(href=url('/reference/attributes.html')) Attributes].
